# Web Beans — Lutece 8

## JspBean (back-office admin)

- MUST have CDI scope + `@Named` + `@Controller` + extend `MVCAdminJspBean`. Without a scope annotation and `@Named`, the page will be BLANK.
- **Scope rule**: `@SessionScoped` if the bean stores per-user state as instance fields (working objects like `_task`, filters, multi-step context). This is the typical case for CRUD beans. Note: with `@Inject @Pager IPager` pagination state is managed automatically — no manual `_strCurrentPageIndex`/`_nItemsPerPage` fields needed. `@RequestScoped` if the bean is stateless (no session instance fields).
- Right check: `processController()` calls `init(request, right)` itself with the `@Controller.right` value. Do NOT call `init()` from the JSP or from the bean. `init()` is only for non-MVC beans (portlets, legacy JspBeans).

### `@Controller` attributes (admin, `fr.paris.lutece.portal.util.mvc.admin.annotations.Controller`)

| Attribute | Required | Meaning |
|---|---|---|
| `controllerJsp` | yes | JSP file name, e.g. `"ManageTasks.jsp"` |
| `controllerPath` | yes | JSP folder, e.g. `"jsp/admin/plugins/myplugin/"` |
| `right` | yes | Admin right code checked by `init()`, e.g. `"MYPLUGIN_MANAGEMENT"` |
| `pageTitleProperty` / `pageTitleI18nKey` | no | Default page title |
| `pagePathI18nKey` | no | Default page path |
| `securityTokenEnabled` | no (default `false`) | Enables automatic CSRF handling — set it to `true` (see below) |

```java
@SessionScoped
@Named
@Controller( controllerJsp = "ManageTasks.jsp", controllerPath = "jsp/admin/plugins/myplugin/", right = "MYPLUGIN_MANAGEMENT", securityTokenEnabled = true )
public class TaskJspBean extends MVCAdminJspBean
```

## XPage (front-office site)

- `@Named("plugin.xpage.id")` + `@Controller` + extend `MVCApplication`. The `@Named` value MUST be `<plugin name>.xpage.<application-id>`: the core resolves the CDI bean by that name (`XPageAppService.registerXPageApplication`).
- Declared in plugin.xml `<applications>` with `<application-id>` only, no `<application-class>`.

### `@Controller` attributes (xpage, `fr.paris.lutece.portal.util.mvc.xpage.annotations.Controller`)

| Attribute | Required | Meaning |
|---|---|---|
| `xpageName` | yes | Page name in the URL (`Portal.jsp?page=<xpageName>`) |
| `pageTitleProperty` / `pageTitleI18nKey` | no | Default page title |
| `pagePathProperty` / `pagePathI18nKey` | no | Default page path |
| `securityTokenEnabled` | no (default `false`) | Enables automatic CSRF handling — set it to `true` (see below) |

```java
@SessionScoped
@Named( "myplugin.xpage.tasks" )
@Controller( xpageName = "tasks", pageTitleI18nKey = "myplugin.xpage.tasks.pageTitle", pagePathI18nKey = "myplugin.xpage.tasks.pagePathLabel", securityTokenEnabled = true )
public class TasksXPage extends MVCApplication
```

## Models — MANDATORY

- **NEVER use `getModel()`** — it is deprecated.
- **NEVER use `new HashMap<>()`** to build the model manually.
- **Preferred form**: declare a `Models model` parameter on the `@View`/`@Action` method — the core resolves it (`mvc/binding/ModelsParameterResolver`). Field injection `@Inject private Models _models;` is accepted. Both are the same request-scoped object: `getPage()`/`getXPage()` read it automatically.
- `Models.asMap()` returns an **unmodifiable** map — all `put()` calls go through the `Models` object.

```java
@View( value = VIEW_MANAGE_TASKS, defaultView = true )
public String getManageTasks( Models model, HttpServletRequest request )
{
    model.put( MARK_TASK_LIST, TaskHome.getTasksList( ) );
    return getPage( PROPERTY_PAGE_TITLE_MANAGE_TASKS, TEMPLATE_MANAGE_TASKS );
}
```

## CRUD Lifecycle — Strict Naming

| Method | Role | Returns |
|---|---|---|
| `getManageEntities()` | List view | HTML template |
| `getCreateEntity()` | Create form | HTML template |
| `doCreateEntity()` | Create action | Redirect URL |
| `getModifyEntity()` | Edit form | HTML template |
| `doModifyEntity()` | Edit action | Redirect URL |
| `getConfirmRemoveEntity()` | Confirm dialog | AdminMessage URL |
| `doRemoveEntity()` | Delete action | Redirect URL |

View and action constants share the same value for a given form: `VIEW_CREATE_ENTITY = ACTION_CREATE_ENTITY = "createEntity"`. This is what lets the automatic CSRF token generated by the view match the action (see below).

## Every `do*` Method — Mandatory Order

1. Bind + validate: `@Valid @ModelAttribute Entity entity, BindingResult bindingResult` parameters (or `populate(entity, request)` + `validate(entity)` for legacy beans)
2. On `bindingResult.isFailed()`: `model.put( MVCUtils.MARK_ERRORS, bindingResult.getAllErrors( ) )` and return the form view
3. Business logic: `EntityHome.create(entity)`
4. Redirect: `redirectView(request, VIEW_MANAGE)` — `request` MUST be a method parameter

`BindingResult` API (`mvc/binding/BindingResult.java`): `isFailed()`, `getAllMessages()`, `getAllErrors()`, `getBindingErrors()`, `getValidationErrors()`, `getErrors(param)`. Each `ParamError` exposes `getMessage()` and `getParamName()`. No `hasErrors()` / `getDefaultMessage()` (Spring names).

## CSRF Policy — `securityTokenEnabled = true` (single source of truth)

Set `securityTokenEnabled = true` on every `@Controller` (JspBean and XPage). This is the current core mechanism; manual `MARK_TOKEN` / `validate()` is legacy.

What the core does (`SecurityTokenHandler`, `SecurityTokenFilterAdmin`, `SecurityTokenFilterSite`, `AppTemplateService`):
- **Generation**: on each `@View` (and default view) a token is generated for the action named by `@View.securityTokenAction`, or by the view value when the attribute is empty. `getPage()`/`getXPage()` put it in the model under `_csrftoken` and `AppTemplateService` injects `<input type="hidden" name="_csrftoken">` into every `<form ...>` of the rendered template. Templates need NOTHING.
- **Validation**: the filter validates the token of every POST `@Action` of the controller. GET/HEAD requests are never filtered, so state-changing actions MUST be POST forms. Invalid token → `AccessDeniedException`.
- **Confirm dialogs**: `@View( value = VIEW_CONFIRM_REMOVE, securityTokenAction = ACTION_REMOVE )` generates the token for the remove action; `AdminMessageService` / `SiteMessageService` confirmation pages pick it up from the session.
- **Opt-out per action**: `@Action( value = ACTION_X, securityTokenDisabled = true )` — only for actions that do not change state (AJAX reads, imports validated otherwise).

```java
@View( value = VIEW_CONFIRM_REMOVE_TASK, securityTokenAction = ACTION_REMOVE_TASK )
public String getConfirmRemoveTask( HttpServletRequest request )
{
    UrlItem url = new UrlItem( getActionUrl( ACTION_REMOVE_TASK ) );
    url.addParameter( PARAMETER_ID_TASK, request.getParameter( PARAMETER_ID_TASK ) );
    return redirect( request, AdminMessageService.getMessageUrl( request, MESSAGE_CONFIRM_REMOVE_TASK, url.getUrl( ), AdminMessage.TYPE_CONFIRMATION ) );
}

@Action( ACTION_REMOVE_TASK )
public String doRemoveTask( HttpServletRequest request )
{
    TaskHome.remove( Integer.parseInt( request.getParameter( PARAMETER_ID_TASK ) ) );
    return redirectView( request, VIEW_MANAGE_TASKS );
}
```

**Blind spots of the automatic token** (core 8.0.x, observed on e2e benches), write the controller around them and
cover each one with a negative e2e scenario:
- **An `@Action` also runs on GET, unvalidated.** The filter skips GET while `processController` dispatches the action
  whatever the method, so `ManageX.jsp?action=removeX&id=1` in a link or an `<img>` deletes. Every mutation is a POST
  form (a link to a confirmation `@View`, never to the action), and the negative scenario proves the GET forgery.
- **The default view reached without `?view=` issues no token**: an entry point linked from elsewhere
  (`admin_url`, another plugin) opens the default view bare, and its form then posts no valid token. Redirect the bare
  default view to `?view=<name>`, or link the entry points with their view.
- **A `page` parameter switches the check off**: `SecurityTokenHandler` takes its XPage branch as soon as the request
  carries `page=`, finds no XPage of that name and lets the request through, even under `/jsp/admin/`. A forged admin
  POST with `page=x` added runs without token. A back-office form must never name a field `page` (use `id_page`),
  and the negative scenarios forge that case too.
- **Registration is lazy**: a controller's actions are registered for validation on its first `processController`
  call after the server starts (`MVCAdminJspBean`); until then the filter does not know the path and a POST
  **without** token is executed. A negative scenario must hit a controller nobody opened since boot, not only a warm
  one.
- **One token per rendered view**: `@View.securityTokenAction` names a single action. A screen with several mutations
  (move, copy, enable per row) routes them through one dispatching action posted with an operation field, or through
  confirmation views, each carrying its own token.
- **The token is injected into every `<form>`, GET ones included** (pagination selects, search forms): it then lands
  in URLs and access logs. Keep search and pagination forms out of pages that issue a mutation token, or accept and
  say so.
- **A multipart form drops the query string**: the admin multipart handler builds the parameters from the body only,
  so `action=`/`view=` must be hidden fields of the form, not in its `action` URL, and one token exists per view:
  one form, one action; a secondary button goes through a confirmation view.

Forbidden with `securityTokenEnabled = true`: `SecurityTokenService.MARK_TOKEN` in the model, `getToken()`, `validate()`, `@Inject SecurityTokenService` in the bean. Keep them ONLY in non-MVC beans (no `@Controller`), where `getSecurityTokenService()` (inherited from `AdminFeaturesPageJspBean`) is the accessor.

## Errors from a view

`addError( … )` then `redirectView( … )` carries the message only from an `@Action` (the redirect goes through the
alert-message handler). From a `@View` the message is lost: the next page shows no error. A view that cannot render
(unknown or non-numeric id) answers an `AdminMessage` of type STOP (`redirect( request,
AdminMessageService.getMessageUrl( request, key, AdminMessage.TYPE_STOP ) )`); an action that refuses its input
uses `addError` + `redirectView` back to the form.

## Pagination (list views)

Use `@Inject @Pager IPager` for CDI-managed pagination (recommended). The `@Pager` qualifier configures paginator name, list bookmark, default items per page, and base URL. Call `_pager.withListItem(list).populateModels(request, model, getLocale())`. For AJAX tables, add a `@ResponseBody` endpoint and use the `paginationAjax` macro. See `/lutece-patterns` skill §5 for full details.
